well throughout this campaign i've said  that the new prime minister's sole  economic priority must be to bear down  ruthlessly on inflation and support  people with their bills now i'm simply  not going to promise anything that  undermines my ability to deliver on that  and i get that alternative plans of  permanent tax cuts funded by borrowing  may sound attractive at first but if  they risk stoking inflation and actually  do nothing to help poorer people and  pensioners then they actually are going  to be bad for everyone ultimately  governing is about choosing and i think  people know that if something sounds too  good to be true it probably is  we know the energy price cap is due to  go up on friday labor and many energy  bosses are calling for it to be frozen  people are frankly terrified about how  they're going to afford to live over  this winter you don't support that  freeze i understand but what concrete  measures do you have in mind to actually  help people with this what are you going  to do about the price of energy well as  chancellor i introduced a wimpful tax on  the energy companies because i believe  that is the fair and right thing to do  in these circumstances and i announced  support for people this autumn winter  but this situation has got worse since  then so as prime minister i would go  further i'd cut vat on energy bills to  provide some help to everyone but i want  to provide direct financial assistance  to two other groups of people those on  the lowest incomes and pensioners  because those people will need extra  help this autumn winter and i know  things are difficult and i want them to  be reassured that with me as prime  minister they will get the help that  they need people are trying to budget  now though as you say the situation has  changed a lot can you give us any more  specific details about the kind of  support you would offer to the most  vulnerable  well i've set out a clear framework for  how to provide that support i would cut  vat off energy bills to coincide with  the increase in the price cap that's  something we can do very quickly but  beyond that for two groups of people for  pensioners and for those on the lowest  incomes i will make sure that they get  direct extra financial support over the  autumn and winter to help them with  those bills because i know this is the  number one concern people have  my approach is the right one it provides  direct help to the most vulnerable in  our society and doesn't do things that  risk making the situation worse and  actually permanent tax cuts funded by  borrowing may sound attractive but if  they stoke inflation and do nothing to  help these groups of people who i know  need help then actually no one benefits  from that and and that's why i think my  plan is the right one my approach is the  right one and as prime minister i can  deliver that for the british people  on monday nearly 1300 migrants arrived  across the channel seems that they're a  threat of being deported to rwanda isn't  working what's your response to that and  what needs to be done about it well  earlier in this campaign i talked about  some of the emergencies facing our  country and illegal migration is one of  the ones that i listed that i would  tackle as a priority as prime minister  because i think it's wrong and i came  here or my family came here because our  country has a proud history of welcoming  migrants but that has to be done legally  has to be done fairly and when people  are exploiting the system that's wrong  and it must be stopped i've set out a  detailed plan for how to get to grips  with it it involves removing the  european definition of asylum moving to  a different international definition  that will help us remove people i will  do whatever it takes to make the rwanda  plan work because we must have control  of our borders and as prime minister  that's what i will deliver  latest forecasts suggesting inflation  could reach 18 next year you previously  said that we can take measures to  control inflation you've talked about  how liz trust is going to add to  inflation but what concrete measures can  you tell us about that you would bring  in to actually curb inflation  well i think we need to let the bank of  england get on with its job with  interest rates and i'm worried quite  frankly by reports from others that they  want to curb the independence of the  bank of england i think that would be a  mistake and i think it would spook  international investors into the united  kingdom it would be bad for all of us  secondly i want to make sure that we  increase the supply of things if you  want to bring the price down of them  that means moving people off welfare and  into work to ease some of the pressures  in our labor market it means increasing  the supply of homegrown energy and food  to reduce inflationary pressures there  and embarking on a big program of energy  efficiency to help people insulate their  homes which reduces their bills and  helps us get to net zero and increases  our energy security but last but not  least we can't put fuel on the fire and  that's why i'm worried about plans that  would pump tens of billions of pounds of  borrowed money into an economy at a time  when inflation and interest rates are  already rising i think that's a big  gamble i don't want to put fuel on the  fire i don't think that's the right  approach i think it's risky and it's not  something i will do as prime minister  did you do enough to control inflation  when you were a child  well of course when i was chancellor i  was also dealing with a pandemic and  people will remember it's probably the  first time that they got to know who i  was at the beginning of the pandemic and  i'm proud of my record because we were  forecast to have unemployment spiraling  into the millions and because of the  interventions that i put in place i  managed to safeguard our economy through  the biggest shock it had experienced in  300 years and actually we're in more  resilient shape now as a result of the  actions i took but now  we do have to make sure that we bring  inflation down which is my priority and  we support the most vulnerable with the  cost of living this autumn and winter  that's my plan and if i'm elected as  prime minister that's what i'll deliver  and comment just like you one very final  question just for the pool i mean people  are very worried about what they're  going to do this winter we've talked  about the energy price cap inflation and  so on energy chiefs saying this race is  going on too long we need a prime  minister in place now who can take  decisions this country needs should you  not be standing aside for the good of  the country  i'm going out and about across the  country talking to our members about my  plan because i believe it's the right  plan for our country and it's a  resonating message with people  everywhere i go i'm doing multiple  events as you can see here today and i  know that i can act very quickly if i'm  uh selected as prime minister people saw  that in the pandemic i had to move very  fast to help get our country through  some very difficult times i will do that  again if i'm prime minister people have  seen my record they know that i've got  the experience they know i've got the  ability to help get the country through  difficult times and at the moment i'm  focused relentlessly on making sure that  my plan is the one that carries the most  weight in the country thank you very  much indeed thank you appreciate your  time
 

